@BBuf Thanks for flagging. I checked both failed runs: it's the speed check in `test_dllm_batching_fdfo.py::test_bs_1_speed`, not a crash. On H100 the bs=1 decode speed came out at ~240 tok/s (240.16 on `h100-computable-e-gpu-3`, 239.72 on `h100-computable-e-gpu-5`) against a threshold of 250.

It's unrelated to this PR: the test runs LLaDA2.0-mini with the flashinfer backend on NVIDIA, and this PR only touches `aiter_backend.py`, which is AMD-only. The threshold was already relaxed to 200 on main in #47989, but this branch was 110 commits behind, so CI was still using 250.

I've updated the branch with main (head `2a0a5ebbf2` now includes #47989), and CI is re-running.

@chaojhou thanks, you are right: only FlyDSL `optimize` and `forge_runner` parse the stdout result block; fusion reads `--result-json` through `_read_result_json` and shares nothing with `kernelforge.loop.api` but the argv builder.

The description now says so in all four places that counted fusion as a reader:
- the summary: three workflows built the argv, and the two stdout readers parsed the result their own way;
- the `kernelforge.loop.api` bullet: the argv builder serves all three, the result framing serves the two stdout readers, and fusion still reads `--result-json`;
- Preserved contracts: "the two stdout readers (FlyDSL `optimize` and `forge_runner`)";
- Observable effect: the malformed-result WARNING is logged for both stdout readers, and fusion is unaffected.

No code changed. The branch also has `main` merged in since your review (#2263, the conflicts were imports and the acyclic baseline), with CI rerunning on it.

@chaojhou Thanks — both are fixed in 34b4d996d, on top of a merge of current main (7905008eb).

**1. Stale `model_info` on resume.** Took both parts of your suggestion. `describe_model` is gone and the two call sites are back to `summarize_model_config`, so `model_info` is again the `config.json` summary only. The target's name and path come from `SharedState.model_name` / `model_path`, exported at warm time through `_STATE_PARAMS` into `SpecialistPromptInputs.model_name` / `model_path` — the existing SharedState → specialist channel that already carries framework, tp, precision and the workload shape — so there is no persisted copy to go stale. `summarize_model_config` now always writes `mtp_layers` (0 when none), as it already does `is_moe`, and `_mtp_step` reads an absent key as "not recorded; read it from config.json". A pre-PR DeepSeek-V3 summary now gets the target from SharedState and that MTP step instead of "No MTP"; `test_focus_asks_for_the_mtp_count_when_the_summary_does_not_record_it` covers both an unreadable config and a summary that predates `mtp_layers`.

@ZhengGong-amd this brings back the two `SpecialistPromptInputs` fields your first review asked to remove. Carrying the identity inside `model_info` is what made it a persisted second copy of `SharedState.model_name` / `model_path`; it now has one route from its owner, and `model_info` holds no identity.

**2. Tags-only delegates.** `warm_specialist_params` now resolves the domain with `dispatch_domain(params)` after the gap backfill — the same resolution the runner's `_prepare` does (`domain_for_tag(dispatch_domain_key(params))`) — so a `domain` key, a tag key, a KB-anchor tag, and an anchor backfilled from a gap's `domain_hint` all get `model_info`. `test_dispatch_tells_the_specialist_the_session_model` is parametrized over those four shapes; with the raw-`domain` check restored, three of the four fail.

Also fixed while there: `_MODEL_INFO_DOMAINS` had been inserted between `_STATE_PARAMS` and its `#:` doc comment; it now sits above it with its own.

The description is updated (supporting changes, tests, and the size table re-measured against main at 43712699b: `runner.py` / `_prepare` and `warm_specialist_params` each grow by 2 lines and are now listed under `baseline-raise`). On the merged tree, 1198 tests across every suite touching `model_info`, `warm_specialist_params`, `summarize_model_config`, static recon, `SpecialistPromptInputs` or speculative decoding pass; ruff and lint-imports are clean. `run-e2e` is still set, so e2e reruns on this commit.
